#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287782 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054636 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fca5176-1359-5ad8-b012-592ffa9b20b5" :knowledge-point/title "Cartesian Products With Three Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19f7aafa-48da-5a9a-8c4e-2db357b4d84d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285018} {:answer/id #uuid "80d7f3e3-c855-5354-90bd-482a3a29e8fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285972} {:answer/id #uuid "ac89eae1-7a02-5a20-8e6e-78af9e33af11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286365} {:answer/id #uuid "dd658f81-0ef3-5121-9761-f8e1ea69f717" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286802} {:answer/id #uuid "eb312ff4-8754-5481-8f71-6c35c1ce24a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286941}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19f7aafa-48da-5a9a-8c4e-2db357b4d84d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285018} :answer-field/id #uuid "a96dd43b-afeb-5167-94cd-5072d0236b88" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287492}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6e14f595-c123-5de8-8c3d-4afeb7095ae5" :question/math-academy-id "q-181977" :question/problem "If $A={4,8}$ and $B={x,y,z},$ which of the following elements belong to $A\\times (A\\times B)?$\n\n- $(4,(8,z))$\n- $(8,(8,x))$\n- $(8,4,y)$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nIn this case, $A\\times B$ consists of every combination of ${4,8}$ with ${x,y,z},$ as shown in the schematic below.\n\n![](images/72/72feee192b949dcce7b0f10f4e1d772c809bcf73de6c66f15c7de75a4a1ff672.png)\n\nHence, the set $A\\times B$ is given by\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(4,x),(4,y),(4,z),(8,x),(8,y),(8,z)}.\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n|A\\times B|=|A|\\cdot |B|=2\\cdot 3=6.\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian product\n\n$$\nA\\times (A\\times B)\n$$\n\nconsists of every combination of $A$ and $A\\times B,$ as shown in the schematic below.\n\n![](images/1a/1ae41857d4aee82a2a4ad838c20bbe72b05a1505a5f4df3aa4a86fda0fff0612.png)\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n|A\\times (A\\times B)|=|A|\\cdot |A\\times B|=2\\cdot 6=12.\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, we see that both $(4,(8,z))$ and $(8,(8,x))$ are in $A\\times \\text{\\(}A\\times B).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287783 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054633 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b304527c-5cdc-57df-b118-e555f6f8fa5c" :knowledge-point/title "Creating a Schematic for a Cartesian Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e49d978-c74c-5053-916f-da9e91cd465a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285652} {:answer/id #uuid "66e4ba73-987f-567f-b03a-1a4788dc8fb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285726} {:answer/id #uuid "755e41be-466c-54bc-814e-78ded8856969" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285872} {:answer/id #uuid "85fff24e-7ebb-59f7-a8eb-f0c5e73cb140" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286020} {:answer/id #uuid "df7f61d1-2b33-51b8-ac5f-36611eacb313" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286827}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66e4ba73-987f-567f-b03a-1a4788dc8fb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285726} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7cd1dc3-fc29-53e9-9ccf-cd951624e0eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287650}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "16f7d99c-b5b7-5532-9552-dac57833067d" :question/math-academy-id "q-181978" :question/problem "![](images/d6/d6ee87bd861491394c6643679f62cb4b94436bf2777bd2436b3169e779736e2c.png)\n\nUsing the visual schematic above for the Cartesian product ${A}^{2}$ for $A={h,k},$ determine which of the following statements are true.\n\n- ${h}^{2}\\in {A}^{2}$\n- $(h,k)\\in {A}^{2}$\n- $(k,k)\\in {A}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "The set ${A}^{2}$ is equivalent to the Cartesian product $A\\times A.$\n\nThe Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nTo complete our schematic of $A\\times A,$ we write an ordered pair whose first coordinate is the corresponding element of the first set $(A)$ and the second coordinate is the corresponding element of the second set $(A)$ at each intersection.\n\n![](images/50/507f8f6306c309d5e894cfb1dabfc2019f371911f7878b196e1f329f1b37dfa6.png)\n\nAccording to our diagram:\n\n- ${h}^{2}\\in {A}^{2}\\,\\times $\n\n- $(h,k)\\in {A}^{2}\\,✓$\n\n- $(k,k)\\in {A}^{2}\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287789 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054634 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6128eca-7f76-5386-9d67-d81db2943eeb" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Cartesian Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b32442c-08ba-5d50-8370-b4f7bb2844ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285346} {:answer/id #uuid "45feb55c-e7bb-5ec4-bd19-6ccbce9dd40d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285428} {:answer/id #uuid "5938940a-29bf-5630-bd36-aefb018067d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285604} {:answer/id #uuid "701d78ff-0afb-5095-8913-1e2f45fedc33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285824} {:answer/id #uuid "f258fef0-e676-5a91-90bd-704c2797ac3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287010}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45feb55c-e7bb-5ec4-bd19-6ccbce9dd40d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285428} :answer-field/id #uuid "26fdac77-4c39-59a1-bd83-eb6b67c2475f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287230}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4b374349-dba8-556c-833d-28419c5d38a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-182011" :question/problem "For the sets $X=\\{x,z\\},$ $Y=\\{a,b\\},$ and $Z=X\\times Y,$ determine which of the following statements are true.\n\nI. $(a,x)\\in Z$\n\nII. $(z,a)\\in Z$\n\nIII. $(z,z)\\in Z$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $X\\times Y$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $X$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $Y.$\n\nIn this case, the schematic for $X\\times Y$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/b7/b73e7aeb017bd9f7c50f7d110c7933763834eb391ac4a3e97d25f4e587a8a3d4.png)\n\nHence, the set $Z=X\\times Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nZ={(x,a),(x,b),(z,a),(z,b)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287794 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054634 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6128eca-7f76-5386-9d67-d81db2943eeb" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Cartesian Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "185bd08d-601b-5996-aa46-119809fc0e9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285004} {:answer/id #uuid "6b25454a-379d-514d-9495-201f4fb2f25e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285772} {:answer/id #uuid "d8592ff8-f46b-59c6-8155-1f6eed956f9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286757} {:answer/id #uuid "e113c0d2-d5af-5904-9bb3-8262221f8793" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286835} {:answer/id #uuid "efc94a9f-df3c-552c-910e-e0e725d8b58f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286986}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b25454a-379d-514d-9495-201f4fb2f25e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285772} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5236379-f233-5a90-9c31-1e6ca30f35bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287549}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "700eeff1-f170-54bf-ba40-d6fc6885d99f" :question/math-academy-id "q-182020" :question/problem "For the sets $Z={t,u}$ and $Y=Z\\times Z,$ determine which of the following statements are true.\n\n- $(u,t)\\in Y$\n- $(u,t,u)\\in Y$\n- $({t}^{2},{u}^{2})\\in Y$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $Z\\times Z$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $Z$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $Z.$\n\nIn this case, the schematic for ${Z}^{2}=Z\\times Z$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/fa/fa14e54c0518f8f5e64d3ccb4f725bd0b3804fb197d7b90320398ebbf09d68d4.png)\n\nHence, the set $Y=Z\\times Z$ is given by\n\n$$\nY={(t,t),(t,u)(u,t),(u,u)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290259 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064166 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbee870b-4a67-5ccb-b274-45fac627be6e" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Given Critical Point Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b7362ff-a436-5cb6-9204-3010f624b437" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288118} {:answer/id #uuid "453e0607-d5d2-538c-a3c2-e1a9e1e7a2f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288503} {:answer/id #uuid "68d8bff0-cd45-560e-83b3-2bf7502b8689" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288766} {:answer/id #uuid "7984ae98-d07e-5a40-926d-f57b4c7945eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288869} {:answer/id #uuid "cbd2e152-3e72-5590-a016-28add032a5e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289383}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7984ae98-d07e-5a40-926d-f57b4c7945eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288869} :answer-field/id #uuid "78352eff-37c5-5d6a-bb54-329102c8e509" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289992}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "206675d2-b06e-5deb-b30d-ae541d334107" :question/math-academy-id "q-20983" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2}+24x.$ Given that ${f}^{′}(2)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{″}(2)=0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=2$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=2$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $x=2$ is a stationary point of $f(x).$ We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2}+24x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=6{x}^{2}-24x+24.\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=12x-24.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(2) & =12\\cdot 2-24=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is zero, we conclude that the test is inconclusive, we need to use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's  find other stationary points. These points are the solution of the equation ${f}^{′}(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{2}-24x+24 & =0 \\\\ 6(x-2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the only stationary point is $x=2.$\n\nLet's choose auxiliary points from each side of the stationary point and evaluate the first derivative at them. We take the auxiliary points $x=1,3$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(1) & =6(1)^{2}-24(1)+24=6>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & =6(3)^{2}-24(3)+24=6>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince the first derivative does not change the sign at $x=2$, we conclude that $f(x)$ has no relative extrema at this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290271 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105893 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca93cd65-65e3-521e-a1e3-2721f45fb6a6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Coordinates of an Image Formed by a Singular Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "263a9ab0-0b2b-5f98-b08a-75e35a2b5402" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288283} {:answer/id #uuid "2da74dc2-9cc7-5196-b1ef-75839c2e3d62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288338} {:answer/id #uuid "8107db4e-6fee-5bbf-b090-80b86be25791" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288902} {:answer/id #uuid "f3795410-4f80-5f8e-952a-15e81fad9a52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289640} {:answer/id #uuid "fb629756-ffa8-5582-a501-069e5ac41c24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289690}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "263a9ab0-0b2b-5f98-b08a-75e35a2b5402" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288283} :answer-field/id #uuid "270e9673-2204-5d44-bf5e-040805890aed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289821}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b1e5b57c-3102-59cd-a153-f7966b8c71f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-21066" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nA triangle $S$ has vertices at $(0,2)$, $(-1,1)$ and $(3,1).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the action of $T?$\n\n- $(12,6)$\n- $(4,2)$\n- $(4,6)$" :question/worked-solution "To find the image of $S$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}12 & 4 & 12 \\\\ 6 & 2 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(12,6)$, $(4,2)$ and $(12,6).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290273 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105893 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca93cd65-65e3-521e-a1e3-2721f45fb6a6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Coordinates of an Image Formed by a Singular Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5fd03eed-5160-504d-92b3-9fc4a9929faa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288701} {:answer/id #uuid "b5444ca4-73fe-5b10-9da7-36fef390b188" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289230} {:answer/id #uuid "c3cd9c3c-09f6-5fe6-b2f9-0e4a932e7829" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289325} {:answer/id #uuid "ce0e1181-c323-5aff-a724-e6ea077e013b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289407} {:answer/id #uuid "fab1568a-9d80-55a3-b772-c365ab8fdb93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289682}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce0e1181-c323-5aff-a724-e6ea077e013b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289407} :answer-field/id #uuid "db73644e-7f77-54f1-91db-8d3791e00546" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290199}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9622b4aa-6606-5dbb-91d5-63f920f6e03c" :question/math-academy-id "q-21068" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nA triangle $S$ has vertices at $(1,2)$, $(-1,2)$ and $(-1,0)$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the action of $T?$\n\n- $(16,8)$\n- $(8,2)$\n- $(-4,4)$" :question/worked-solution "To find the image of $S$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$ \n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}16 & 8 & -4 \\\\ 8 & 4 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(16,8)$, $(8,4)$ and $(-4,-2).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290309 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064165 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae256269-0bec-5d57-ac4a-eb0b07926f6f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points and Then Classifying Them Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43c12eb3-55f1-5542-9408-dd989e93c601" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288494} {:answer/id #uuid "496b50b2-2381-5c0a-a5ec-57b315ce03f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288541} {:answer/id #uuid "a6aed7f1-0992-5dbd-9e5b-68b34683d114" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289136} {:answer/id #uuid "e671bd82-67fb-5076-a0c6-d9a760f7d607" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289562} {:answer/id #uuid "fae129e1-58dc-5648-9f67-66fc8378cba1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289684}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6aed7f1-0992-5dbd-9e5b-68b34683d114" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289136} :answer-field/id #uuid "c77a77dc-758e-557f-a490-659cf8cc382d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290162}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "48699d28-6189-5c8c-ac56-4e78a856e54a" :question/math-academy-id "q-21500" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=-3{x}^{5/2}+5{x}^{3/2}+15$ on $(0,\\infty ),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=1$ is a critical point of $f(x)$\n- ${f}^{″}(1)<0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=1$" :question/worked-solution "We need to identify whether $x=1$ is a critical point. If it is, we then need to classify it using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)=-3{x}^{5/2}+5{x}^{3/2}+15,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-3\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}{x}^{3/2}+5\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}{x}^{1/2}=-\\frac{15}{2}{x}^{3/2}+\\frac{15}{2}{x}^{1/2},\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{15}{2}{x}^{3/2}+\\frac{15}{2}{x}^{1/2} & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{15}{2}{x}^{1/2}(x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have a critical point at $x=1.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at this critical point. Computing ${f}^{″}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=-\\frac{15}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}{x}^{1/2}+\\frac{15}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2}=-\\frac{45}{4}{x}^{1/2}+\\frac{15}{4}{x}^{-1/2},\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{″}(x)$ at $x=1$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(1)=-\\frac{45}{4}(1)^{1/2}+\\frac{15}{4}(1)^{-1/2}=-\\frac{15}{2}<0.\n$$\n\nThe second derivative is negative at $x=1,$ so this critical point is a relative *maximum*. \n\nTherefore, comparing our results with the given statements, we conclude that:\n\n- $x=1$ is a critical point of $f(x)\\,✓$\n\n- ${f}^{″}(1)<0\\,✓$\n\n- $f(x)$ has a relative *maximum* at $x=1\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290310 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064165 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae256269-0bec-5d57-ac4a-eb0b07926f6f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points and Then Classifying Them Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14a643fc-b865-5596-8f40-9701cd340073" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288174} {:answer/id #uuid "710f998a-91e7-56d8-bc7d-70a603ad78c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288819} {:answer/id #uuid "cc280798-2e6b-5ad9-add1-5725b15e8da0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289389} {:answer/id #uuid "d3960910-1995-56c5-b003-a5bddf4a4554" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289433} {:answer/id #uuid "feca9e5b-047c-5d2d-861d-f5037ef6a710" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289717}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "feca9e5b-047c-5d2d-861d-f5037ef6a710" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289717} :answer-field/id #uuid "e49dfb32-835f-597e-bf8e-067b148c9379" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290214}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2b9fc172-9b76-5d55-bed8-430ab7579f16" :question/math-academy-id "q-21504" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=-6{x}^{2}+36x-48,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=3$ is a critical point of $f(x)$\n- ${f}^{″}(3)<0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=3$" :question/worked-solution "We need to identify whether $x=3$ is a critical point. If it is, we then need to classify it using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)=-6{x}^{2}+36x-48,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-12x+36,\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-12x+36 & =0 \\\\ -12(x-3) & =0 \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have a critical point at $x=3.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at this critical point. Computing ${f}^{″}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=-12,\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{″}(x)$ at $x=3$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(3)=-12<0.\n$$\n\nThe second derivative is negative at $x=3,$ so this critical point is a relative *maximum*. \n\nTherefore, comparing our results with the given statements, we conclude that:\n\n- $x=3$ is a critical point of $f(x)\\,✓$\n\n- ${f}^{″}(3)<0\\,✓$\n\n- $f(x)$ has a relative *maximum* at $x=3\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290363 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064165 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae256269-0bec-5d57-ac4a-eb0b07926f6f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points and Then Classifying Them Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "172441b4-d434-5708-a4a6-ef5a3be8199c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288186} {:answer/id #uuid "23e10618-8683-5f0d-95cc-80d118d3ff12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288259} {:answer/id #uuid "57f0af3a-fe31-5542-a4b2-9b4f3ee54df4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288644} {:answer/id #uuid "6a4873fc-0aa3-5e53-a988-44237de9e1e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288783} {:answer/id #uuid "d33d546d-1cbe-56d3-a508-703423641292" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289431}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57f0af3a-fe31-5542-a4b2-9b4f3ee54df4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288644} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e068c60-158a-57de-beed-8c11e09a8d10" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289973}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f2b37341-4b83-58cd-8078-617f2c8f3e30" :question/math-academy-id "q-22011" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=12{x}^{3}-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=-6$ is a critical point of $f(x)$\n- ${f}^{″}(-6)<0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=-6$" :question/worked-solution "We need to identify whether $x=-6$ is a critical point. If it is, we then need to classify it using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)=12{x}^{3}-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5},$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=36{x}^{2}-{x}^{4},\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}36{x}^{2}-{x}^{4} & =0 \\\\ -{x}^{2}({x}^{2}-36) & =0 \\\\ -{x}^{2}(x+6)(x-6) & =0 \\\\ x & =-6,0,6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have critical points at $x=-6,0,6.$ We're interested in the critical point $x=-6.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at this critical point. Computing ${f}^{″}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=72x-4{x}^{3},\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{″}(x)$ at $x=-6$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(-6)=432>0.\n$$\n\nThe second derivative is positive at $x=-6,$ so this critical point is a relative *minimum*. \n\nTherefore, comparing our results with the given statements, we conclude that:\n\n- $x=-6$ is a critical point of $f(x)\\,✓$\n\n- ${f}^{″}(-6)<0\\,\\times $\n\n- $f(x)$ has a relative *minimum* at $x=-6\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290364 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064165 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae256269-0bec-5d57-ac4a-eb0b07926f6f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points and Then Classifying Them Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "060f212a-f478-5952-bd6f-7bf7f70e10d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288077} {:answer/id #uuid "062633c1-4565-58df-8006-f852b8d73ae6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288080} {:answer/id #uuid "5f2a9169-4d25-5c53-85fa-0c1c8b93a42b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288696} {:answer/id #uuid "72388a42-fb86-50b2-96bf-d70a348cf382" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288827} {:answer/id #uuid "ff535e30-6ddf-5160-bc91-b1ca4d83420f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289722}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72388a42-fb86-50b2-96bf-d70a348cf382" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288827} :answer-field/id #uuid "183b3786-4390-561d-92eb-b9e0cde29dee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289789}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "247bae04-64ae-5dcd-80c8-d60760babf45" :question/math-academy-id "q-22015" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=3{x}^{5}-10{x}^{3}+9,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=\\sqrt{2}$ is a critical point of $f(x)$\n- ${f}^{″}(\\sqrt{2})<0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=\\sqrt{2}$" :question/worked-solution "We need to identify whether $x=\\sqrt{2}$ is a critical point. If it is, we then need to classify it using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)=3{x}^{5}-10{x}^{3}+9,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=15{x}^{4}-30{x}^{2},\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15{x}^{4}-30{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 15{x}^{2}({x}^{2}-2) & =0 \\\\ 15{x}^{2}(x+\\sqrt{2})(x-\\sqrt{2}) & =0 \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt{2},0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have critical points at $x=\\pm \\sqrt{2},0.$ We're interested in the critical point $x=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at this critical point. Computing ${f}^{″}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=60{x}^{3}-60x,\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{″}(x)$ at $x=\\sqrt{2}$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(\\sqrt{2})=60(\\sqrt{2})^{3}-60\\sqrt{2}=60\\sqrt{2}>0.\n$$\n\nThe second derivative is positive at $x=\\sqrt{2},$ so this critical point is a relative *minimum*. \n\nTherefore, comparing our results with the given statements, we conclude that:\n\n- $x=\\sqrt{2}$ is a critical point of $f(x)\\,✓$\n\n- ${f}^{″}(\\sqrt{2})>0\\,\\times $\n\n- $f(x)$ has a relative *minimum* at $x=\\sqrt{2}\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290365 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064166 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbee870b-4a67-5ccb-b274-45fac627be6e" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Given Critical Point Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c2a2818-f16a-5ca3-a33e-8cb2e00a9471" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288120} {:answer/id #uuid "2d7c83d5-55ec-506f-87b9-7ecf2d7cbda9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288336} {:answer/id #uuid "58f5aa1b-cd48-548e-9168-e16448b4505c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288654} {:answer/id #uuid "62842dd6-40b1-5bf6-aa93-c01869c60a63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288720} {:answer/id #uuid "6342d3c9-7eaa-52a1-930c-3b1ad9bb7e70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288725}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c2a2818-f16a-5ca3-a33e-8cb2e00a9471" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288120} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7f6d5e2-3ce5-5110-a9e4-a19c7f7c0f86" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290163}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cfe05852-ce59-5bf2-a32a-e36a882c576e" :question/math-academy-id "q-22016" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=3{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}-30{x}^{2}+36x+12.$ Given that ${f}^{′}(1)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{″}(1)<0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=1$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=1$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $x=1$ is a stationary point of $f(x).$ We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function $f(x)=3{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}-30{x}^{2}+36x+12,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=12{x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}-60x+36.\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=36{x}^{2}+24x-60.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1) & =36(1)^{2}+24(1)-60=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is zero, we conclude that the test is inconclusive, we need to use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's  find other stationary points. Solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12{x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}-60x+36 & =0 \\\\ 12({x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-5x+3) & =0 \\\\ 12(x-1)({x}^{2}+2x-3) & =0 \\\\ 12(x-1)(x-1)(x+3) & =0 \\\\ x & =-3,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=-3$ and $x=1.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-4,0,2$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-4) & =12(-4-1)^{2}(-4+3)=-300<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =12(0-1)^{2}(0+3)=36>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =12(2-1)^{2}(2+3)=60>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-3)$ | $-3$ | $(-3,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-4$ |  | $0$ |  | $2$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |  | $↗$ |\n\nAt $x=1,$  the first derivative does not change the sign. Therefore, $x=1$ is neither a local minimum nor a local maximum.\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290373 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064165 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae256269-0bec-5d57-ac4a-eb0b07926f6f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points and Then Classifying Them Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "64787f14-ad0d-5964-aa88-af6f60cccc76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.ty